<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-intro-blinky">
    <title>GPIO and Blinky: Your First Embedded Program</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Welcome to ENGS 28!  The tradition in programming is to start with
            <q>Hello, World</q>—a minimal program that proves everything is working.
            In embedded systems, the equivalent is <term>Blinky</term>: a program that
            makes an LED flash on and off.  By the end of this chapter you will
            understand every line of a working Blinky program, including the hardware
            it runs on, the registers it writes to, and the bit-manipulation idioms
            used throughout the course.
        </p>
        <p>
            The material here corresponds to Days 1, 1x, and 2 of the course.
            The hardware is the <term>STM32 Nucleo-64</term> development board, and
            the microcontroller on it is the <term>STM32C031C6</term>, based on an
            Arm<registered /> Cortex<registered />-M0+ processor core.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Describe the key hardware components of the STM32 Nucleo-64 board.</li>
                <li>Explain how GPIO pins are organized into ports and addressed by bit number.</li>
                <li>Write and explain a complete Blinky program, including the ODR, MODER,
                    and RCC registers.</li>
                <li>Apply bit-twiddling idioms to set, clear, toggle, and test individual bits.</li>
                <li>Wire an external LED on a breadboard and measure its voltage and current.</li>
                <li>Use <c>delay_ms()</c> to produce accurate time delays.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-nucleo-board">
        <title>The STM32 Nucleo-64 Board</title>
        <idx><h>STM32 Nucleo-64</h></idx>
        <idx><h>STM32C031C6</h></idx>

        <p>
            In this course we use the <term>STM32 Nucleo-64</term> development board.
            The board's main chip is the <term>STM32C031C6</term> microcontroller,
            which contains a 32-bit Arm Cortex-M0+ processor, 32 KB of flash memory,
            12 KB of RAM, and a set of on-chip peripherals (timers, UART, I<sup>2</sup>C,
            ADC, and more)—all on a single integrated circuit smaller than a postage stamp.
            The Nucleo board surrounds that chip with everything needed to program and
            power it from a laptop: a USB interface, voltage regulators, an Arduino-compatible
            header, and a few buttons and LEDs for quick experiments.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-nucleo-board-photo">
            <caption>The STM32 Nucleo-64 development board.  The small square chip near the
            center is the STM32C031C6 microcontroller—the focus of this course.  The board
            also includes a USB interface chip (ST-LINK/V2) for programming and debugging,
            voltage regulators, a reset button, an on-board user LED, and Arduino-compatible
            header connectors along both long edges for connecting external components.</caption>
            <image source="slides/Day01-Intro-Blinky/slide08_8334ac38.png" width="90%"/>
        </figure>

        <p>
            <xref ref="fig-nucleo-board-labeled"/> shows the layout of the board from above.
            Running along the left and right edges are the two rows of
            <em>Arduino-compatible header pins</em>—these are where you will plug in jumper
            wires to connect LEDs, buttons, and sensors.  The small green LED near the top
            right of the header area is the <em>user LED</em> we will blink first; the
            black push-button next to it is the <em>reset button</em>.  Near the top of the
            board, flanking the ST-LINK chip, are two additional connectors called
            <em>Morpho connectors</em> that expose more pins—we will not use those.
            The USB-B Mini connector on the top edge carries both power and the
            programming/debugging link to your laptop.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-nucleo-board-labeled">
            <caption>Top view of the STM32 Nucleo-64 board.  The Arduino-compatible digital
            I/O header pins run along both long edges.  The on-board user LED and user button
            are near the top of the right-hand header.  The ST-LINK USB programming interface
            occupies the top portion of the board; the STM32C031C6 microcontroller is the
            small square chip in the center.</caption>
            <image source="slides/Day01-Intro-Blinky/slide09_898fa71b.png" width="65%"/>
        </figure>

        <p>
            The STM32C031C6 has 48 physical pins (<xref ref="fig-stm32-pinout"/>).
            Not all of those pins are general-purpose I/O—some carry power, ground,
            the reset signal, or the clock crystal connections.  Of the programmable
            I/O pins, they are organized into three <term>GPIO ports</term> named
            A, B, and C.  Each port can have up to 16 pins, identified by a number
            0–15.  A pin is therefore named by its port letter and bit number:
            <em>PA5</em> means Port A, bit 5; <em>PB10</em> means Port B, bit 10.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-stm32-pinout">
            <caption>Pin diagram of the STM32C031C6 microcontroller (48-pin LQFP package).
            Pins are grouped into three GPIO ports: A, B, and C.  Port A and Port B each
            have 16 programmable pins; Port C has fewer.  The on-board user LED is connected
            to PA5 (physical pin 16).</caption>
            <image source="slides/Day01-Intro-Blinky/slide13_aa5f257b.png" width="75%"/>
        </figure>

        <p>
            The Arduino-compatible header on the Nucleo board labels its pins D0–D15 and
            A0–A5.  These labels are inherited from the Arduino platform and exist only
            to help you plug Arduino shields onto the board.  The microcontroller itself
            has no concept of <q>D13</q>; it knows only Port A, bit 5 (PA5).
            <xref ref="fig-pin-naming"/> illustrates this mapping for the on-board LED.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-pin-naming">
            <caption>Three equivalent names for the same physical connection: Arduino header
            label D13, physical MCU pin 16, and the port/bit address PA5 (Port A, bit 5).
            In code and in the STM32 reference manual, pins are always identified by their
            port and bit number, not by the physical package pin or the Arduino label.</caption>
            <image source="slides/Day01-Intro-Blinky/slide14_03977edf.png" width="75%"/>
        </figure>

        <p>
            The same signal path is shown in circuit form in
            <xref ref="fig-led-pin-connection"/>: the microcontroller's PA5 output is
            routed through a current-limiting resistor to the on-board LED, and the same
            trace also appears on the Arduino header's D13 pin so you can probe it or
            connect an external device.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-led-pin-connection">
            <caption>Partial schematic of the Nucleo board showing how the on-board LED is
            wired.  The LED and its current-limiting resistor are connected between PA5 of
            the STM32C031C6 and ground; the same PA5 signal also reaches Arduino header
            pin D13 on the right edge of the board.</caption>
            <image source="slides/Day01-Intro-Blinky/slide15_41e5a81c.png" width="70%"/>
        </figure>

        <aside>
            <title>What is <q>Arduino</q>?</title>
            <p>
                Arduino is an Italian company that makes low-cost microcontroller boards.
                Their hardware and software are open source, and the form factor of their
                header connectors became a de-facto standard—so many third-party boards,
                including our STM32 Nucleo, replicate the same connector layout.  In this
                course we use the Nucleo's <em>Arduino-compatible headers</em> but we do
                <em>not</em> program in the Arduino language; instead we write C with
                direct register access.
            </p>
        </aside>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-nucleo-board">
            <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-pa5-meaning">
                <statement>
                    <p>What does the pin name PA5 mean on the STM32C031C6?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Port A, bit 5 — the 6th pin of GPIO Port A.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  STM32 GPIO pins are always identified by port
                        letter and bit number (0-indexed).</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Physical package pin number 5 on the chip.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>No.  Physical pin numbers depend on the chip package and
                        are not used in code.  PA5 means Port A, bit 5.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Arduino header pin A5.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>No.  Arduino header pin A5 is an analog input label.
                        PA5 is a different notation meaning Port A, bit 5.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The 5th peripheral register in the GPIOA block.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>No.  PA5 refers to a specific I/O pin, not a register
                        offset within the GPIO structure.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-nucleo-led">
                <statement>
                    <p>The on-board user LED on the Nucleo-64 board corresponds to
                    which GPIO pin on the STM32C031C6?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>PA5 (Port A, bit 5).</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  PA5 is also accessible as Arduino header
                        pin D13 and is physical chip pin 16.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>PC13 (Port C, bit 13).</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>PC13 is the on-board user button, not the LED.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>PB5 (Port B, bit 5).</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Close, but the LED is on Port A, not Port B.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>D13 (Arduino digital pin 13).</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>D13 is the Arduino-header label for the same physical
                        connection, but in STM32 code the pin is always referred to as
                        PA5.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-blinky-program">
        <title>Your First Program: blinky.c</title>
        <idx><h>blinky.c</h></idx>
        <idx><h>GPIO</h><h>ODR</h></idx>
        <idx><h>GPIO</h><h>MODER</h></idx>
        <idx><h>RCC</h><h>IOPENR</h></idx>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Before diving into individual registers, consider what the program must
                accomplish at the hardware level.  There are four logical steps:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Enable the clock to GPIO Port A.</li>
                <li>Configure PA5 as a digital output.</li>
                <li>Set bit 5 high to turn the LED on.</li>
                <li>Clear bit 5 low to turn the LED off.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                Each step corresponds to writing to a specific memory-mapped register.
                The complete <c>blinky.c</c> source is shown below; the four steps
                appear as four register writes.
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "stm32C0xx.h"        // all port/register definitions live here

// Bit 0 of RCC->IOPENR enables the clock for GPIOA
#define GPIOAEN   (1U << 0)   // 0b0000...0000 0001  (32-bit)

// Bit 5 of Port A is wired to the on-board LED
#define LED_PIN   (1U << 5)   // 0b0000...0010 0000

int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR  |= GPIOAEN;    // Step 1: enable clock for GPIOA
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U << 10); // Step 2: configure PA5 as output
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U << 11);//         (write '01' into bits 11:10)

    while (1) {
        GPIOA->ODR |= LED_PIN;  // Step 3: set bit 5  → LED on
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++); // rough delay

        GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_PIN; // Step 4: clear bit 5 → LED off
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++); // rough delay
    }
    return 0; // never reached
}
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The rest of this section explains each register in detail, following
                the order they are discussed in class: ODR first, then MODER, then RCC.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-odr">
            <title>Steps 3 and 4: The Output Data Register (ODR)</title>
            <idx><h>ODR (Output Data Register)</h></idx>

            <p>
                <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR</c> stands for <term>Output Data Register for GPIO Port A</term>.
                It is a 16-bit register—one bit per pin—that determines whether each
                output pin is driven HIGH or LOW.  Writing a <c>1</c> to bit 5 drives
                PA5 to 3.3 V (LED on); writing a <c>0</c> drives it to 0 V (LED off).
            </p>

            <p>
                The simplest approach would be to overwrite the entire register at once:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
GPIOA->ODR = 0b0000000000100000; // sets all pins; only bit 5 is 1
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                This works in isolation but clobbers every other bit—if another output
                pin were already set, this would silently turn it off.  A safer pattern
                uses the <term>bitwise OR-assign</term> operator to set only bit 5 without
                touching any other bit:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
GPIOA->ODR |= (1U << 5);   // set bit 5, leave all others unchanged
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                Here <c>(1U &lt;&lt; 5)</c> shifts a <c>1</c> five places to the left,
                producing the 32-bit value <c>0b…0010 0000</c>.  OR-ing that with the
                current register value sets bit 5 to <c>1</c> while leaving every other
                bit untouched.  Giving the mask a named constant makes the intent clear:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define LED_PIN  (1U << 5)
GPIOA->ODR |= LED_PIN;    // turn LED on  (set   bit 5)
GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_PIN;   // turn LED off (clear bit 5)
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                The second line uses bitwise NOT (<c>~</c>) to flip all bits of
                <c>LED_PIN</c>, producing <c>0b…1101 1111</c>.  AND-ing that mask
                with the register clears bit 5 while leaving all other bits unchanged.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-moder">
            <title>Step 2: The Mode Register (MODER)</title>
            <idx><h>MODER</h></idx>

            <p>
                Before the ODR can drive a pin, that pin must be configured as an output.
                <c>GPIOA-&gt;MODER</c> is the <term>Mode Register</term> for Port A.
                It is a 32-bit register with <em>two bits per pin</em>: bits
                <m>2n+1</m> and <m>2n</m> control pin <m>n</m>.  The four possible
                two-bit patterns are shown in <xref ref="table-moder-encoding"/>.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-moder-encoding">
                <title>MODER bit-pair encoding</title>
                <tabular halign="left">
                    <row header="yes">
                        <cell>Bits [2n+1 : 2n]</cell><cell>Pin mode</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row><cell><c>00</c></cell><cell>Input (reset default)</cell></row>
                    <row><cell><c>01</c></cell><cell>General-purpose output</cell></row>
                    <row><cell><c>10</c></cell><cell>Alternate function</cell></row>
                    <row><cell><c>11</c></cell><cell>Analog</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                To configure PA5 as an output we write <c>01</c> into its two MODER bits.
                Pin 5 uses bits <m>2 \times 5 = 10</m> (the lower bit) and bit 11
                (the upper bit).  We set bit 10 to <c>1</c> and clear bit 11 to <c>0</c>:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
GPIOA->MODER |=  (1U << 10); // set   bit 10 → '1'
GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U << 11); // clear bit 11 → '0'
// Result: bits [11:10] = '01' → PA5 is now a general-purpose output
]]></code></program>

            <aside>
                <title>Why two lines?</title>
                <p>
                    If you only set bit 10 and bit 11 happened to be <c>1</c> already,
                    the two-bit pattern would be <c>11</c> (analog mode) rather than
                    <c>01</c> (output).  Always explicitly clear the upper bit to
                    guarantee the correct mode regardless of the reset state.
                </p>
            </aside>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-rcc">
            <title>Step 1: Enabling the Clock (RCC)</title>
            <idx><h>RCC</h><h>IOPENR</h></idx>
            <idx><h>clock enable</h></idx>

            <p>
                By default, the clock to each GPIO port is <em>gated off</em> to save
                power.  You cannot read from or write to any GPIO register until its
                clock is enabled.  The register that controls GPIO clocks is
                <c>RCC-&gt;IOPENR</c>: the <term>I/O Port Clock Enable Register</term>
                of the <term>Reset and Clock Control</term> (RCC) subsystem.
            </p>

            <p>
                <c>IOPENR</c> is a 32-bit register; bit 0 enables the clock for GPIOA,
                bit 1 for GPIOB, bit 2 for GPIOC, and so on.  To enable GPIOA:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define GPIOAEN  (1U << 0)    // bit 0 → GPIOA clock enable
RCC->IOPENR |= GPIOAEN;       // turn on the GPIOA clock
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                This must appear <em>before</em> any access to <c>GPIOA-&gt;MODER</c>,
                <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR</c>, or any other GPIOA register.  In practice, the
                very first thing <c>main()</c> should do is enable clocks to all
                peripherals it will use.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-blinky-program">
            <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-odr-purpose">
                <statement>
                    <p>What is the purpose of <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR</c>?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>It is the Output Data Register: writing a <c>1</c>
                        to a bit drives the corresponding pin HIGH; writing <c>0</c>
                        drives it LOW.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It configures whether each pin is an input
                        or output.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That is the MODER register.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It enables the clock to Port A.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That is <c>RCC-&gt;IOPENR</c>.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It reads the voltage currently present on
                        an input pin.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Reading input pin voltages uses the IDR
                        (Input Data Register), covered in
                        <xref ref="ch-switches"/>.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-moder-bits">
                <statement>
                    <p>Which MODER bits configure pin PA7 as an output, and what
                    values must they hold?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Bit 14 must be <c>1</c> and bit 15 must be <c>0</c>
                        (pattern <c>01</c> in bits [15:14]).</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct: pin <m>n = 7</m> uses bits
                        <m>2n = 14</m> (lower) and <m>2n+1 = 15</m> (upper).
                        Output mode is <c>01</c>, so bit 14 = 1 and bit 15 = 0.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Bit 7 must be <c>1</c> and bit 6 must be <c>0</c>.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Bits 6 and 7 control pin 3 (since 2×3 = 6).
                        Pin 7 needs bits 14 and 15.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Bit 15 must be <c>1</c> and bit 14 must be <c>0</c>.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Those are the right bits, but the values are swapped.
                        Output mode (<c>01</c>) puts the <c>1</c> in the lower bit (14),
                        not the upper bit (15).</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Bits 13 and 12.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Bits 13 and 12 control pin 6 (2×6 = 12).</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-why-rcc-first">
                <statement>
                    <p>Why must <c>RCC-&gt;IOPENR |= GPIOAEN</c> appear
                    <em>before</em> any write to <c>GPIOA-&gt;MODER</c>?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>The GPIOA peripheral's clock is gated off by
                        default; writing to its registers has no effect until the
                        clock is enabled.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The STM32 gates peripheral clocks to
                        save power; you must turn a peripheral's clock on before using
                        it.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>MODER is a read-only register until IOPENR
                        is set.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>MODER is not read-only, but writing to it before
                        enabling the clock is silently ignored because the peripheral
                        is not yet active.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The processor generates a hard fault if GPIOA
                        is accessed without enabling its clock.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>On the STM32C031C6 the processor will not fault,
                        but the write to MODER will be ignored and the pin will remain
                        in its reset (input) state.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>IOPENR must be set so that the IDE can detect
                        the GPIO port.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>IOPENR is a hardware register, not a software
                        flag.  The IDE is not involved at run time.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-bit-twiddling">
        <title>Bit Twiddling</title>
        <idx><h>bit twiddling</h></idx>
        <idx><h>bitwise operators</h></idx>

        <p>
            The operations used in Blinky—setting and clearing individual bits without
            disturbing the rest of a register—appear in virtually every line of
            embedded-systems code.  Collectively they are called <term>bit twiddling</term>.
            The three fundamental patterns are:
        </p>

        <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Set bit n in register NUM (force that bit to 1):
NUM |= (1U << n);

// Clear bit n in register NUM (force that bit to 0):
NUM &= ~(1U << n);

// Toggle bit n in register NUM (flip 0→1 or 1→0):
NUM ^= (1U << n);
]]></code></program>

        <p>
            The expression <c>(1U &lt;&lt; n)</c> is called a <term>bit mask</term>: it
            has exactly one <c>1</c> bit (at position <m>n</m>) and zeros everywhere
            else.  The <c>U</c> suffix makes it an <em>unsigned</em> 32-bit integer,
            which is important when <m>n</m> equals 31 (shifting into the sign bit of
            a signed <c>int</c> would cause undefined behavior in C).
        </p>

        <p>
            Two additional idioms let you <em>test</em> whether a bit is set without
            modifying the register:
        </p>

        <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Test if bit n is 0:
if ((NUM & (1U << n)) == 0)  { /* bit n is 0 */ }

// Test if bit n is 1 (non-zero):
if ((NUM & (1U << n)) != 0)  { /* bit n is 1 */ }
]]></code></program>

        <p>
            AND-ing a register with a one-bit mask zeroes every bit except the one
            we care about.  The result is either <c>0</c> (bit was <c>0</c>) or a
            non-zero value (bit was <c>1</c>).
        </p>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-bit-twiddling">
            <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-toggle-example">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Suppose <c>NUM = 0b00101101</c> (8-bit example).
                        What is the value of <c>NUM</c> after
                        <c>NUM ^= (1U &lt;&lt; 5)</c>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p><c>0b00001101</c></p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Bit 5 in <c>0b00101101</c> is
                        already <c>1</c>; XOR with <c>1</c> toggles it to <c>0</c>,
                        giving <c>0b00001101</c>.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p><c>0b00101101</c> (unchanged)</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>XOR with <c>1</c> always flips the bit; it does
                        not leave it unchanged.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p><c>0b00111101</c></p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>This would result from setting bit 4.
                        Verify: <c>(1U&lt;&lt;5)</c> = <c>0b00100000</c>.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p><c>0b10101101</c></p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Check the mask position: <c>(1U&lt;&lt;5)</c>
                        sets bit 5, which is <c>0b00100000</c>, not bit 7.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-clear-bit">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write the single C statement that clears bit 3 in a 32-bit
                        register named <c>REG</c> without changing any other bit.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <response/>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-modifying-blinky">
        <title>Modifying Blinky</title>

        <p>
            Now that you understand the complete Blinky program, the best way to
            deepen that understanding is to modify it.  The busy-wait delay loop
            <c>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 100000; i++);</c> provides a rough delay
            whose duration depends on the processor clock speed and compiler
            optimization level.  Changing the loop bound changes the blink rate.
        </p>

        <p>
            When creating a new variation of <c>blinky.c</c>, right-click the file
            in the IDE's Project Explorer and choose <em>Copy</em>, then paste it
            into the same <c>Src</c> folder and assign a new name.
            Because a C project can have only one <c>main()</c> function, you must
            <em>exclude</em> the old file from the build: right-click it, choose
            <em>Resource Configurations → Exclude from Build</em>, and select all
            configurations.
            <xref ref="fig-exclude-from-build"/> shows the IDE after excluding
            <c>blinky.c</c> so that only the new file is compiled.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-exclude-from-build">
            <caption>STM32CubeIDE Project Explorer after excluding <c>blinky.c</c>
            from the build.  The file icon shows a visual indicator that it will be
            skipped by the compiler.  A second file containing the new <c>main()</c>
            function is selected as the active source.  Only one <c>main()</c>
            may exist in the active build at any time.</caption>
            <image source="slides/Day01x-Blinky/slide14_3d6b6f15.png" width="40%"/>
        </figure>

        <exercises xml:id="act-modify-blinky">
            <title>Activity</title>

            <exercise xml:id="act-blinky-rate">
                <title>Varying the Blink Rate</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Make a copy of <c>blinky.c</c> and experiment with the loop
                        bound to produce at least three noticeably different blink rates
                        (slow, medium, fast).  Exclude <c>blinky.c</c> from the build
                        and test each version on the board.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="act-blinky-slow-to-fast">
                <title>blinkySlowToFast.c</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Design and implement <c>blinkySlowToFast.c</c>: the LED blinks
                        slowly at first, with the blink rate steadily increasing until
                        it reaches a fast rate; then the cycle repeats.
                        Write out your approach on paper first, have a classmate review
                        it, then implement and test it.  Aim for clean, readable code
                        with meaningful constant names.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Before diving in, think about these questions: What variable could
                        you change on each cycle to make the delay shorter?  Do you need
                        a nested loop?  An outer loop that modifies the inner bound?
                        How will you detect when <q>fast</q> has been reached and reset
                        back to <q>slow</q>?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-mcu-architecture">
        <title>Inside the Microcontroller</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                So far we have treated registers as magic addresses: write a <c>1</c>
                here and the LED lights up.  This section pulls back the curtain and
                explains <em>how</em> a write in your C program changes a voltage on
                a physical pin.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-how-mcu-runs">
                <caption>Overview of how a C program controls an LED.  The compiler
                translates C into machine instructions and stores them in the chip's flash
                memory.  At run time the CPU repeatedly fetches and executes those
                instructions, eventually writing a value to a memory-mapped GPIO register.
                That register drives the output transistors on the pin, which in turn
                source or sink current through the LED.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide08_799bfba4.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-processor-arch">
            <title>Processor Architecture</title>
            <idx><h>Cortex-M0+</h></idx>
            <idx><h>fetch-decode-execute</h></idx>

            <p>
                The Cortex-M0+ processor contains a set of <term>registers</term>—fast
                storage locations inside the processor itself (<xref ref="fig-m0plus-registers"/>).
                There are 16 general-purpose 32-bit registers (R0–R15), plus a Program
                Counter (PC), a Link Register (LR), and a Stack Pointer (SP).  All
                computation uses these registers; data must be loaded from memory first,
                processed, then written back.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-m0plus-registers">
                <caption>The Cortex-M0+ internal register file.  The processor has
                thirteen general-purpose 32-bit registers (R0–R12) that hold operands
                and results during computation, plus three special-purpose registers:
                SP (stack pointer, R13), LR (link register for return addresses, R14),
                and PC (program counter, R15).  Every GPIO write ultimately passes
                through this register file.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide10_0d9da36e.png" width="40%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                When the CPU executes <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR |= LED_PIN</c>, the underlying
                machine instructions are:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><em>Load</em> the current value of ODR from address <c>0x5000 0014</c>
                    into a CPU register.</li>
                <li><em>OR</em> that value with the LED_PIN mask.</li>
                <li><em>Store</em> the result back to <c>0x5000 0014</c>.</li>
            </ol>

            <p>
                This pattern—<term>fetch, decode, execute</term>—repeats for every
                machine instruction (<xref ref="fig-fetch-execute"/>).  The processor
                fetches the instruction at the address in the PC, decodes what operation
                it encodes, executes it, then increments the PC.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-fetch-execute">
                <caption>The instruction fetch-decode-execute cycle, illustrated for a
                GPIO write.  On each cycle the CPU fetches the next instruction from
                flash at the address in PC, decodes its opcode, executes the operation
                (here a store to address <c>0x5000 0014</c>), and advances PC.
                The AHB/APB bus fabric routes the store to the GPIO peripheral rather
                than to SRAM because the target address falls in the GPIO address
                range.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide11_cb6cab2b.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-memory-mapped-io">
            <title>Memory-Mapped I/O</title>
            <idx><h>memory-mapped I/O</h></idx>

            <p>
                The STM32C031C6 uses <term>memory-mapped I/O</term>: peripheral registers
                live at specific addresses in the same 4 GB address space as flash and
                RAM (<xref ref="fig-memory-map"/>).  From the CPU's perspective, writing
                to a GPIO register looks identical to writing to a RAM variable—but instead
                of storing a value in a memory cell, it changes the state of a hardware
                circuit.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-memory-map">
                <caption>Simplified memory map of the STM32C031C6, showing the three
                regions that matter for Blinky.  Flash memory (holding program code and
                constants) occupies addresses starting at <c>0x0800 0000</c>.  SRAM
                (stack, local variables, and global data) starts at <c>0x2000 0000</c>.
                GPIO peripheral registers live at <c>0x5000 0000</c> and beyond—far
                enough from SRAM that an accidental out-of-bounds RAM access cannot
                inadvertently toggle a hardware pin.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide12_6f1ab27a.png" width="45%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The GPIOA base address is <c>0x5000 0000</c>.  ODR is at offset
                <c>0x14</c> within the GPIO structure, giving absolute address
                <c>0x5000 0014</c>.  In the STM32 header file, <c>GPIOA</c> is a C
                pointer cast to the structure's base address:
            </p>

            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Inside stm32c031xx.h (simplified):
#define GPIOA  ((GPIO_TypeDef *) 0x50000000UL)

typedef struct {
    __IO uint32_t MODER;   /* offset 0x00 */
    __IO uint32_t OTYPER;  /* offset 0x04 */
    __IO uint32_t OSPEEDR; /* offset 0x08 */
    __IO uint32_t PUPDR;   /* offset 0x0C */
    __IO uint32_t IDR;     /* offset 0x10 */
    __IO uint32_t ODR;     /* offset 0x14 */
    /* ... */
} GPIO_TypeDef;
]]></code></program>

            <p>
                So <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR = value</c> is exactly equivalent to writing
                to address <c>0x5000 0014</c>.  The hardware detects that address on
                the bus and routes the write to the GPIO output latch rather than to RAM.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-odr-address">
                <caption>Computing the absolute address of <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR</c>.  The
                GPIOA peripheral is mapped to base address <c>0x5000 0000</c>, and ODR
                sits at offset <c>0x14</c> within the <c>GPIO_TypeDef</c> structure,
                giving absolute address <c>0x5000 0014</c>.  Writing to that address
                via the memory bus causes the GPIO hardware to latch the new value
                and update the output transistors on all 16 Port A pins.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide15_4c3a0ecd.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-gpio-pin-circuit">
            <title>How a GPIO Output Pin Works</title>
            <idx><h>push-pull output</h></idx>

            <p>
                Each GPIO output pin contains a complementary pair of transistors
                connected between the 3.3 V supply and ground.  In <term>push-pull</term>
                mode (the default when MODER = <c>01</c>), writing a <c>1</c> to the
                ODR bit turns on the upper PMOS transistor and turns off the lower NMOS
                transistor, pulling the pin to 3.3 V.  Writing a <c>0</c> does the
                reverse, pulling the pin to 0 V.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-gpio-output-circuit">
                <caption>Simplified schematic of a GPIO output cell in push-pull mode.
                A complementary transistor pair (PMOS on top, NMOS on bottom) is
                controlled by the ODR bit for that pin.  Writing <c>1</c> turns the
                PMOS on and NMOS off, connecting the pin to VDD (3.3 V); writing
                <c>0</c> turns the NMOS on and PMOS off, pulling the pin to GND.
                The MODER register chooses between this output driver and an input
                buffer (not shown).</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide14_bca9b3c1.png" width="65%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-mcu-architecture">
            <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-memory-mapped">
                <statement>
                    <p>What does <q>memory-mapped I/O</q> mean for the STM32C031C6?</p>
                </statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Peripheral registers (like GPIO ODR) are placed at
                        specific addresses in the CPU's address space, so reading and writing
                        them uses the same load/store instructions as accessing ordinary
                        RAM.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The CPU does not need special I/O instructions;
                        a store to <c>0x5000 0014</c> changes the GPIO output just as a store
                        to a RAM variable changes that variable.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The microcontroller maps the contents of RAM to GPIO
                        pins at startup.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>No.  Memory mapping refers to the address layout of the
                        address space, not to copying RAM contents to pins.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>GPIO registers are stored in flash memory alongside
                        program code.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>No.  Flash is at <c>0x0800 0000</c>; GPIO registers
                        are at <c>0x5000 0000</c>—separate address regions.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The IDE maps source-code line numbers to machine
                        instruction addresses.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That is debug-symbol mapping, a completely different
                        concept.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-fetch-execute">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        List the three steps in the fetch-decode-execute cycle and
                        briefly describe what happens at each step for the instruction
                        <c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR |= LED_PIN</c>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <response/>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-external-leds">
        <title>External LEDs on the Breadboard</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                The on-board LED is convenient for software testing, but the whole point
                of a microcontroller is to control external devices.  In this section you
                will wire an LED on a breadboard, drive it from a GPIO pin, and use a
                digital multimeter to measure the voltages and currents in the circuit.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-breadboard-setup">
            <title>Setting Up the Breadboard</title>
            <idx><h>breadboard</h></idx>
            <idx><h>digital multimeter (DMM)</h></idx>

            <p>
                Your breadboard has two power rails running along each long edge.
                Your TA will pre-wire the power rails to the Nucleo's 3.3 V and GND
                headers—leave those wires in place all term.
                <xref ref="fig-nucleo-breadboard"/> shows the basic connection.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-nucleo-breadboard">
                <caption>The Nucleo board connected to a breadboard at the start of Lab 1.
                Short red and black jumper wires link the Nucleo's 3.3 V and GND header
                pins to the breadboard's positive and negative power rails, respectively.
                Leave these wires in place for the entire term; all components you build
                will draw power from these rails.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide18_7ffed5f7.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                To build the LED circuit, choose a red or green LED and a 220 Ω resistor
                (color code: red–red–black–black for the 5-band version).  Wire the circuit
                as shown in <xref ref="fig-external-led-circuit"/>: the GPIO pin connects
                through the resistor to the LED anode (+, longer lead), and the LED cathode
                (−, shorter lead) connects to the ground rail.  Always wire and verify the
                circuit before applying power.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-external-led-circuit">
                <caption>An external LED wired on the breadboard and driven from Arduino
                header pin D13 (PA5).  The 220 Ω resistor (connected between the GPIO
                pin and the LED's anode, the longer lead) limits current to roughly
                10–15 mA.  The LED's cathode (shorter lead) connects to the ground
                rail.  The on-board LED and this breadboard LED both blink because they
                share the same PA5 signal.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide23_17f1e348.png" width="60%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The digital multimeter (<xref ref="fig-dmm-photo"/>) is an essential
                tool for verifying circuits.  In this lab you will use it to measure
                DC voltages.  Always set the dial to the appropriate range (20 V DC for
                3.3 V circuits) before touching the probes to the circuit, and turn it
                off when finished to preserve the battery.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-dmm-photo">
                <caption>The digital multimeter (DMM) from your lab kit.  The rotary
                dial selects the measurement type and range; for 3.3 V DC circuits,
                turn it to the 20 V DC position.  Plug the black probe into the COM
                jack and the red probe into the VΩmA jack.  Always turn the dial to
                OFF when you are finished to avoid draining the battery.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide19_4462fc0a.png" width="35%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-led-measurements">
            <title>Measuring Voltage and Current</title>
            <idx><h>LED forward voltage</h></idx>
            <idx><h>Ohm's Law</h></idx>

            <p>
                With the LED circuit powered and the LED lit, measure three voltages
                (<xref ref="fig-led-measurements"/>):
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><m>V_{CC}</m>: from the power rail to ground (should be approximately 3.3 V).</li>
                <li><m>V_F</m>: the forward voltage across the LED (anode to cathode).</li>
                <li><m>V_R</m>: the voltage across the resistor (from LED cathode to ground).</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Kirchhoff's voltage law requires <m>V_{CC} = V_F + V_R</m>.
                Once you have <m>V_R</m>, Ohm's Law gives the current through the
                circuit: <m>I_F = V_R / R</m>.  Compare your measured <m>V_F</m>
                with the value in the LED datasheet—typical forward voltages are about
                2.0 V for red LEDs and 2.1 V for green LEDs.  Then replace the 220 Ω
                resistor with a 1 kΩ resistor and repeat the measurements; observe how
                the current and brightness change.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-led-measurements">
                <caption>The three voltages to measure in the LED circuit, shown with the
                DMM probe positions.  Place probes across the entire circuit for
                <m>V_{CC}</m> (supply to ground), across the LED for <m>V_F</m>
                (anode to cathode), and across the resistor for <m>V_R</m>.
                Kirchhoff's voltage law requires <m>V_{CC} = V_F + V_R</m>;
                Ohm's Law then gives current <m>I = V_R / R</m>.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide21_d8ae16aa.png" width="65%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-new-pin">
            <title>Using a Different GPIO Pin</title>

            <p>
                Blinky used PA5 (D13) because the on-board LED is wired there.  For
                external LEDs you can use any available GPIO output pin.  Move the LED's
                anode wire from D13 to D4 (<xref ref="fig-blinky-d4"/>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-blinky-d4">
                <caption>The breadboard circuit after moving the LED's anode wire from
                D13 to D4.  The resistor and LED remain in place; only one jumper wire
                is rerouted.  Every register reference in the firmware that named Port A
                or bit 5 must be updated to Port B and bit 10 to match the new
                physical connection.</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide25_e6bdef76.png" width="60%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                To determine which GPIO port and bit correspond to D4, consult the Nucleo
                pinout diagram (<xref ref="fig-nucleo-pinout"/>).  D4 connects to
                PB10—Port B, bit 10.  The firmware changes from Blinky are:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Enable the GPIOB clock: set bit 1 (not bit 0) of <c>RCC-&gt;IOPENR</c>.</li>
                <li>Configure PB10 as output: write <c>01</c> to bits 21:20 of
                    <c>GPIOB-&gt;MODER</c>.</li>
                <li>Drive the pin via bit 10 of <c>GPIOB-&gt;ODR</c>.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                The on-board LED (PA5) will no longer blink once you switch to PB10;
                only the external breadboard LED will.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-nucleo-pinout">
                <caption>Nucleo-64 pinout reference, showing each Arduino header label
                and its corresponding STM32 port and bit number.  Use this whenever you
                move a wire to a different header pin and need to know which
                <c>GPIOX-&gt;MODER</c> bits to configure and which <c>GPIOX-&gt;ODR</c>
                bit to drive.  For example, Arduino D4 maps to PB10 (Port B, bit 10).</caption>
                <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide26_995cec21.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-delay-ms">
        <title>A Better Delay: <c>delay_ms()</c></title>
        <idx><h>delay_ms()</h></idx>
        <idx><h>ES28.h</h></idx>

        <p>
            The busy-wait loop <c>for (int i = 0; i &lt; 100000; i++);</c> produces
            an imprecise delay whose actual duration depends on the compiler optimization
            level and the processor clock speed.  It also gives the reader no intuition
            about how long the pause actually is.
        </p>

        <p>
            Starting with Day 2, use the course-provided <c>delay_ms()</c> function
            instead.  Its implementation lives in <c>sysinit.c</c> inside your
            <c>mylib</c> folder.  To make the function prototype visible, replace the
            include line at the top of your source file:
        </p>

        <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Before (Day 1):
#include "stm32C0xx.h"

// From Day 2 onward — use this instead:
#include "ES28.h"   // includes stm32C0xx.h AND the prototype for delay_ms()
]]></code></program>

        <p>
            With that change, you can write readable, self-documenting delays:
        </p>

        <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"

#define GPIOAEN  (1U << 0)
#define LED_PIN  (1U << 5)

int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR  |= GPIOAEN;
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U << 10);
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U << 11);

    while (1) {
        GPIOA->ODR |= LED_PIN;   // LED on
        delay_ms(1000);           // wait exactly 1 second

        GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_PIN;  // LED off
        delay_ms(500);            // wait 0.5 second
    }
    return 0;
}
]]></code></program>

        <p>
            For the voltage-measurement exercise below, slow the blink rate to about
            2 seconds per phase—slow enough for the DMM display to settle and be read
            while the LED is in each state.
        </p>

        <exercises xml:id="act-dmm-voltage">
            <title>Activity</title>
            <exercise xml:id="act-blinky-dmm">
                <title>Measuring GPIO Output Voltage with the DMM</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Modify your Blinky program to blink at roughly 2 seconds on,
                        2 seconds off using <c>delay_ms(2000)</c>.  Connect the red
                        DMM probe to the GPIO output pin (or the anode of the LED) and
                        the black probe to ground.  Set the dial to the 20 V DC range.
                    </p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Record the voltage when the LED is on (HIGH state).
                            Compare with <m>V_{OH}</m> (output high voltage) specified
                            in the STM32C031C6 datasheet.</li>
                        <li>Record the voltage when the LED is off (LOW state).
                            Compare with <m>V_{OL}</m>.</li>
                        <li>Replace the 220 Ω resistor with a 1 kΩ resistor.  Calculate
                            the new current using Ohm's Law.  Does the LED appear brighter
                            or dimmer?  Why?</li>
                    </ol>
                    <p>
                        Remember to turn the DMM dial to OFF when you are finished.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-lab1">
        <title>Lab 1: Three-LED Experiments</title>
        <idx><h>Lab 1</h></idx>

        <p>
            Lab 1 extends the single-LED Blinky to a three-LED circuit
            (<xref ref="fig-three-leds"/>).  Wire three LEDs (using a mix of red and
            green) through 220 Ω resistors to three separate GPIO output pins.
            Each sub-task asks you to write a separate C file implementing a different
            blinking pattern.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-three-leds">
            <caption>Three-LED circuit for Lab 1, with one red and two green LEDs
            (or the reverse—mix colors as available).  Each LED has its own 220 Ω
            resistor and connects to a separate GPIO output pin.  Build incrementally:
            get the first LED blinking before adding the second, and the second before
            adding the third.</caption>
            <image source="slides/Day02-Blinky(2)/slide37_ca307cb5.png" width="55%"/>
        </figure>

        <p>
            Start Lab 1 in a new project (copy your TemplateProject and rename the
            copy <c>Lab1</c>).  Download <c>template.c</c> from Canvas, place it in
            the project's <c>Src</c> folder, and rename it to your first target filename.
            The template includes a header comment block—fill in your name, assignment,
            a table of I/O pins and what they connect to, and a revision history for
            every file you submit.
        </p>

        <p>
            Build and test incrementally: get one LED working before adding the second
            and third.  Use descriptive <c>#define</c> names for every pin and timing
            constant, and keep track of your changes in comments.
        </p>

        <exercises xml:id="act-lab1">
            <title>Lab 1 Activities</title>

            <exercise xml:id="act-blinky-sim">
                <title>blinkySIM.c — Simultaneous Blink</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write <c>blinkySIM.c</c>: all three LEDs blink on and off
                        simultaneously at the same rate.  All three should turn on
                        together, pause, turn off together, pause, and repeat.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Hint: if two LEDs are on the same GPIO port, you can set (or
                        clear) both bits with a single OR (or AND) operation rather than
                        two separate register writes.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="act-blinky-seq">
                <title>blinkySEQ.c — Sequential Blink</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write <c>blinkySEQ.c</c>: the three LEDs blink in sequence—only
                        one is lit at any given time.  The pattern cycles
                        LED 1 → LED 2 → LED 3 → LED 1 → <ellipsis/> continuously.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Once <c>blinkySIM.c</c> is working, copy it to
                        <c>blinkySEQ.c</c> and modify the on/off logic to produce the
                        sequential pattern.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="act-blinky-cnt">
                <title>blinkyCNT.c — Binary Counter</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write <c>blinkyCNT.c</c>: the three LEDs display a 3-bit binary
                        count, cycling through 000, 001, 010, 011, 100, 101, 110, 111,
                        000, <ellipsis/> with a short pause between each step.  Designate
                        which LED represents the least significant bit and which represents
                        the most significant bit.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Hint: a loop variable incrementing from 0 to 7 can be used to
                        drive the three LEDs.  Use bit-twiddling expressions to extract
                        each bit of the counter and map it to the appropriate GPIO pin.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <aside>
            <title>AI Policy for ENGS 28</title>
            <p>
                You are welcome to use the course's HiTA tool (integrated in Canvas)
                for help.  Before asking for assistance, engage in genuine problem-solving
                on your own—productive struggle is where most learning happens.
                If you use any AI tool, disclose it in your code comments, rigorously
                test and understand the output, and be prepared to explain every line.
                All comments in submitted code must be in your own words.
            </p>
        </aside>
    </section>

</chapter>
